# Semantic neighbours

**Semantic neighbours** is a future lens: a query that, placed on any card,
lists the cards closest to it in meaning that it does not link yet. It turns
the gaps of the net into a visible list. It is a design and does not run on
the current HQL; this card must not be rendered.

## What it would do

The relations on a card are written by hand, so a card links only what its
author thought of. Two cards can say nearly the same thing in different words
and never meet. This lens ranks all cards by closeness of meaning to the body
of the current card, removes the ones already linked, and shows the rest as
candidates for a new relation.

The result is a proposal and never an edit. A score may suggest that two
concepts belong together; a person decides whether they do and which relation
holds. That is the same division of labour as in [[human-in-the-loop]].

## The design

```hql
import future

linked = this | uplinks | map(e => e.target)
cards
| semantic(this.body)
| filter(h => h.value.name != this.name and not contains(linked, h.value.name))
| take(5)
| map(h => {card: h.value, score: h.score})
```

## What current HQL lacks

- `this`, the card a cell is written in. Today a cell has to name its card.
- `semantic` over a question that was not pinned when the embedding index was
  built. Today it answers only questions embedded in advance.

Until then the runnable stand-in is the word-overlap query
`lexical-neighbours.hql` in the query library.

## Relations

- It proposes links between cards by [[analogy]] {relation: uses} of meaning, where declared relations rely on the author.
- Its output is material for [[revision]] {relation: uses} of the net.
- It keeps the decision with a person, as [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: uses} requires.
- It serves the question [[ai-support-in-hql]] {relation: answers}.
